WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:08.250
In a typical C program, a parent process
might need to send a continuous stream of

2
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:13.950
data to a child process. But sometimes,
instead of finishing the task, the data flow

3
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.830
suddenly halts. The program completely
freezes in place and the terminal stops

4
00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.560
responding. This is a deadlock.

5
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:26.310
It happens when a program that should complete
its execution ends up waiting forever

6
00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:28.030
for an event that will never occur.

7
00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.820
If we check the terminal output, we can
see the system eventually had to intervene,

8
00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.860
killing the blocked program after
a timeout with exit status 124.

9
00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:42.490
So why does a perfectly valid stream of
bytes between two related processes result

10
00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.380
in a permanent system freeze?

11
00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:48.400
To diagnose this freeze, we have to
look below the privilege boundary.

12
00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.250
This boundary separates our programs,
running in user mode, from the operating

13
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:57.550
system's kernel. When a program uses the
pipe system call, it creates a circular

14
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.530
buffer inside the kernel. This buffer acts
as a continuous byte stream, moving data

15
00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:05.240
strictly in order without
any message boundaries.

16
00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:10.240
The kernel then hands exactly two file
descriptors back to the process in user mode.

17
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:14.460
One descriptor is exclusively for reading,
and the other is strictly for writing.

18
00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:17.410
The pipe provides a direct,
physical bridge for the data.

19
00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:22.230
However, the operating system requires
you to manually and strictly manage these

20
00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:25.080
file descriptors for the
bridge to function safely.

21
00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:29.610
When the parent process calls fork, it
duplicates itself, creating an identical

22
00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:32.290
child process alongside it in user mode.

23
00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.860
The child inherits
everything from the parent.

24
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.330
That means both processes now hold their
own independent pairs of read and write

25
00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:42.090
descriptors, pointing to the
exact same kernel buffer.

26
00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:45.220
This introduces the golden rule of pipes.

27
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.840
Data must flow in only one direction.

28
00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:52.330
To enforce this, each process must actively
sever the connections it does not intend

29
00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:57.170
to use. In a standard setup, the parent uses
the close function on its read end, and

30
00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:59.500
the child uses close on its write end.

31
00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:04.190
Closing these unused descriptors is the
only way the kernel knows the true, one-way

32
00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:05.390
direction of the data stream.

33
00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:10.530
With the pipe set up properly, the parent
process begins using the write system call

34
00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:12.980
to rapidly pump bytes into the channel.

35
00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.120
But this kernel buffer has
a strict physical limit.

36
00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:21.520
On Linux, the default capacity
is exactly 65,536 bytes.

37
00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:25.910
Once that 64-kilobyte capacity
is full, the kernel steps in.

38
00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:30.490
It physically blocks the parent process
from writing any more data until the child

39
00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:32.390
performs a read to free up space.

40
00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:36.480
Eventually, the child process
finishes its task and exits.

41
00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:41.250
When that happens, there are exactly zero
read descriptors left open anywhere in the

42
00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:43.800
system. The kernel detects this absence.

43
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.540
If the parent tries to write again, the
kernel immediately raises a SIGPIPE signal,

44
00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.940
safely terminating the parent
process with exit status 141.

45
00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:55.830
These capacity limits and fatal
signals are not random errors.

46
00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:59.350
They are the strict rules the kernel
enforces to gracefully tear down the

47
00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.060
communication channel. So let's
go back to our initial problem.

48
00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:07.010
Why did our first program freeze indefinitely
instead of safely receiving that

49
00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:10.960
SIGPIPE signal? The culprit is
a single missing line of code.

50
00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:14.640
The parent process forgot to call
close on its own read descriptor.

51
00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:18.330
Because this specific descriptor remains
open, the kernel follows its rules

52
00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:22.880
literally. It believes a valid reader
still exists, the parent itself.

53
00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:26.830
The parent rapidly fills
the 65,536-byte buffer.

54
00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:31.010
Once full, the kernel blocks the parent
forever, waiting for a reader that will

55
00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:32.840
never arrive to clear the space.

56
00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:36.460
A single, unclosed file descriptor
creates a phantom reader.

57
00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:40.390
This small oversight turns a built-in
safety mechanism into a permanent system

58
00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.720
deadlock. Let's do a quick review.

59
00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:45.840
What is the exact capacity
of a pipe on Linux?

60
00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:50.220
65,536 bytes. Second question.

61
00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:54.000
What signal is raised if a process
writes to a pipe that has no readers?

62
00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:57.040
SIGPIPE, causing exit status 141.

63
00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:02.240
Now open animation A5.2 in the
course hub and run lab TD4.
